if(off + 4 >= lines[0].end)
    return FALSE;
```
So `off + 4 < lines[0].end`, meaning `off + 3 <= lines[0].end - 1`. Then `off += 2`, so `off + 1 <= lines[0].end - 1`, meaning `off + 1 < lines[0].end`. Then `md_scan_for_html_closer` with `len=3`: `off + 3 <= lines[0].end`? Since `off + 1 < lines[0].end`, we have `off + 3 < lines[0].end + 2`. This could be `off + 3 > lines[0].end` if `off + 1 >= lines[0].end - 1`. But `off + 1 < lines[0].end` means `off + 1 <= lines[0].end - 1`, so `off + 3 <= lines[0].end + 1`. So `off + 3` could be `lines[0].end + 1`, which is > `lines[0].end`. In that case, the inner loop condition `off + len <= lines[0].end` would be false, and we'd go to the next line. That's fine.

